cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or refer to these results, please cite the paper below."
title: "Comparator construction can reverse the apparent advantage of peak-token MS/MS models over binned baselines"
abstract: >-
  Code, analyses and manuscript for a controlled study of how much of a
  peak-token model's apparent advantage over a binned MS/MS baseline belongs to
  the comparator rather than to the representation. On a public-source corpus of
  414,992 spectra from GNPS, MassBank and MoNA spanning 66 instrument types,
  evaluated on 10,058 scaffold-disjoint held-out structures under three seeds,
  supplying the binned baseline with the acquisition covariates the peak-token
  arms already received raises it from 0.1130 to 0.1418 macro AUPRC, and an
  architecture-specific hyperparameter search raises it to 0.2601, reversing the
  ordering. The same search budget applied to the peak-token arm's own capacity
  parameters leaves it at 0.1573. Because the optimised baseline carries 6.2
  times the parameters, this does not establish that binned representations are
  superior. Within a fixed peak-token encoder, restoring full mass precision
  contributes +0.0142, the study's one single-factor intervention.
